ut_tokens": 5044}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-29T08:57:35.698Z", "request_id": "req_011CgWFSnN3z2B3cZ3zwSLwv", "thinking_duration_ms": 61882}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 0}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 1, "content_block": {"type": "text", "text": ""}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgWFSnoqYFiv2dwrffLhP", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "My estimated top half is candidates 7, 5, 3 and 4. These figures come from rough estimates done in my head, not a full day-by-day simulation, so the lower-confidence picks could be wrong.\n\n**What drives the result.** The plans are fixed in advance and never react to what actually happens. Leftover stock carries forward, while shortages turn into lost sales. So any steady over-ordering keeps piling up, and you pay for it in storage and spoiled stock. Under-ordering costs 11 per unit short (the 7 penalty plus the 4 margin lost). The first days are short for every plan, because nothing ordered can arrive before day 4.\n\n**Each candidate's ordering level.** I compared order sizes with the expected demand they have to cover. The forecast times 1.046 gives that demand.\n\n- **Candidate 7** orders every 2 days, about 1.1× the forecast, so roughly 5% above expected demand. Stock builds slowly. Shortages are rarer and storage stays moderate. This is the best.\n- **Candidate 5** orders at the same level as 7, shifted by one day. It also places one extra small order on day 0, which costs one more 56 fee. It is nearly as good as 7.\n- **Candidate 3** orders exactly the forecast, about 4.5% below expected demand. It saves on storage but loses some sales to shortages. It is close to 7.\n- **Candidate 4** orders every 3 days, slightly above expected demand. It pays 4 fewer daily fees, saving 224. Stock sits on the shelf longer, costing about 420 more in storage. Net, it is about 200 worse than a similar 2-day plan.\n- **Candidate 6** orders every day at about expected demand. It saves about 420 on storage but pays 13 more daily fees, costing 728. Net, it is about 300 worse. This puts it close to 4, which is why both get probabilities near 0.5.\n- **Candidate 2** has item 1 roughly right, but item 0 at about 1.3× the forecast. The surplus of item 0 grows by about 8 units every 2 days. That brings storage costs of about 900 and units that are paid for but never sold.\n- **Candidate 0** orders about 1.25× the forecast for both items. Item 1 piles up by about 11 units per cycle and pushes stock towards the 172-unit warehouse limit. Item 0 grows and spoils too. This is the worst of the 2-day plans.\n- **Candidate 1** orders every 4 days at about 0.9–0.92× expected demand. Shortages cost about 980 and the extra storage about 840. Its 6 fewer daily fees save only 336. It also comes close to the warehouse limit when item 1 arrives.\n\n**Robustness to random demand.** Being slightly above expected demand (7 and 5) works best, because a shortage costs much more than a day of storage. Plans that are well above (0 and 2) fall further behind as stock accumulates. Plans below demand (1, and to a lesser degree 3) lose more on days when demand comes in high."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 5044, "cache_read_input_tokens": 886, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 5044}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_managem